      reg_item    : "FilterAdministratorToken"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.224 WN10-SO-000250"
      info        : "User Account Control must, at minimum, prompt administrators for consent on the secure desktop.

GROUP ID: V-220945RULE ID: SV-220945r958518

User Account Control (UAC) is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. This setting configures the elevation requirements for logged on administrators to complete a task that requires raised privileges."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> \'User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for administrators in Admin Approval Mode\'

to \'Prompt for consent on the secure desktop\'.'
      reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CCI|CCI-001084,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-220945r958518_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000250,Vuln-ID|V-220945"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "2"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"
      reg_item    : "ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type            : AUDIT_POWERSHELL
          description     : "System is a member of a domain"
          value_type      : POLICY_TEXT
          value_data      : "True"
          powershell_args : "(Get-WmiObject -Class Win32_ComputerSystem).PartOfDomain"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <if>
          <condition auto:"WARNING" type:"AND">
            <custom_item>
              type            : AUDIT_POWERSHELL
              description     : "Readers"
              value_type      : POLICY_TEXT
              value_data      : "^Manual Review Required$"
              powershell_args : "$output=(Get-ChildItem -Path HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Cryptography\Calais\Readers | Select-Object Name); if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($output)) { 'No entries found' } else { $output };"
              severity        : MEDIUM
            </custom_item>

            <custom_item>
              type            : AUDIT_POWERSHELL
              description     : "SmartCards"
              value_type      : POLICY_TEXT
              value_data      : "^Manual Review Required$"
              powershell_args : "$output=(Get-ChildItem -Path HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Cryptography\Calais\SmartCards | Select-Object Name); if ([string]::IsNullOrEmpty($output)) { 'No entries found' } else { $output };"
              severity        : MEDIUM
            </custom_item>
          </condition>

          <then>
            <report type:"PASSED">
              description : "1.225 WN10-SO-000251"
              info        : "Windows 10 must use multifactor authentication for local and network access to privileged and nonprivileged accounts.

GROUP ID: V-220946RULE ID: SV-220946r958484

Without the use of multifactor authentication, the ease of access to privileged and nonprivileged functions is greatly increased.

All domain accounts must be enabled for multifactor authentication with the exception of local emergency accounts.

Multifactor authentication requires using two or more factors to achieve authentication.

Factors include:

 - Something a user knows (e.g., password/PIN);
 - Something a user has (e.g., cryptographic identification device, token); and
 - Something a user is (e.g., biometric).

A privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user.

Network access is defined as access to an information system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a network (e.g., local area network, wide area network, or the internet).

Local access is defined as access to an organizational information system by a user (or process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a direct connection without the use of a network.

The DoD CAC with DoD-approved PKI is an example of multifactor authentication.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000106-GPOS-00053, SRG-OS-000107-GPOS-00054, SRG-OS-000108-GPOS-00055

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
              solution    : "For nondomain-joined systems, configuring Windows Hello for sign-on options is suggested based on the organization's needs and capabilities."
              reference   : "800-171|3.5.3,800-171r3|03.05.03,800-53|IA-2(1),800-53r5|IA-2(1),CCI|CCI-000765,CN-L3|7.1.2.7(b),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ITSG-33|IA-2(1),LEVEL|2M,NESA|T5.4.2,NIAv2|AM36,NIAv2|VL3c,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-220946r958484_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000251,SWIFT-CSCv1|1.2,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-220946"
              see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
              show_output : YES
            </report>
          </then>
        </if>
      </then>

      <else>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.225 WN10-SO-000251"
          info        : "Windows 10 must use multifactor authentication for local and network access to privileged and nonprivileged accounts.

GROUP ID: V-220946RULE ID: SV-220946r958484

Without the use of multifactor authentication, the ease of access to privileged and nonprivileged functions is greatly increased.

All domain accounts must be enabled for multifactor authentication with the exception of local emergency accounts.

Multifactor authentication requires using two or more factors to achieve authentication.

Factors include:

 - Something a user knows (e.g., password/PIN);
 - Something a user has (e.g., cryptographic identification device, token); and
 - Something a user is (e.g., biometric).

A privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user.

Network access is defined as access to an information system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a network (e.g., local area network, wide area network, or the internet).

Local access is defined as access to an organizational information system by a user (or process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a direct connection without the use of a network.

The DoD CAC with DoD-approved PKI is an example of multifactor authentication.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000106-GPOS-00053, SRG-OS-000107-GPOS-00054, SRG-OS-000108-GPOS-00055"
          solution    : "For nondomain-joined systems, configuring Windows Hello for sign-on options is suggested based on the organization's needs and capabilities."
          reference   : "800-171|3.5.3,800-171r3|03.05.03,800-53|IA-2(1),800-53r5|IA-2(1),CCI|CCI-000765,CN-L3|7.1.2.7(b),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ITSG-33|IA-2(1),LEVEL|2M,NESA|T5.4.2,NIAv2|AM36,NIAv2|VL3c,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-220946r958484_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000251,SWIFT-CSCv1|1.2,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-220946"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
        </report>
      </else>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.226 WN10-SO-000255"
      info        : "User Account Control must automatically deny elevation requests for standard users.

GROUP ID: V-220947RULE ID: SV-220947r1051036

User Account Control (UAC) is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. Denying elevation requests from standard user accounts requires tasks that need elevation to be initiated by accounts with administrative privileges. This ensures correct accounts are used on the system for privileged tasks to help mitigate credential theft."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> \'User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for standard users\'

to \'Automatically deny elevation requests\'.'
      reference   : "800-171r3|03.05.01b.,800-53|IA-11,800-53r5|IA-11,CCI|CCI-002038,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(d),LEVEL|2M,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-220947r1051036_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000255,Vuln-ID|V-220947"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"
      reg_item    : "ConsentPromptBehaviorUser"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.227 WN10-SO-000260"
      info        : "User Account Control must be configured to detect application installations and prompt for elevation.

GROUP ID: V-220948RULE ID: SV-220948r958518

User Account Control (UAC) is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. This setting requires Windows to respond to application installation requests by prompting for credentials."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> \'User Account Control: Detect application installations and prompt for elevation\'

to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CCI|CCI-001084,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-220948r958518_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000260,Vuln-ID|V-220948"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"
      reg_item    : "EnableInstallerDetection"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.228 WN10-SO-000265"
      info        : "User Account Control must only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations.

GROUP ID: V-220949RULE ID: SV-220949r958518

User Account Control (UAC) is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. This setting configures Windows to only allow applications installed in a secure location on the file system, such as the Program Files or the Windows\System32 folders, to run with elevated privileges."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> \'User Account Control: Only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations\'

to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CCI|CCI-001084,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-220949r958518_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000265,Vuln-ID|V-220949"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"
      reg_item    : "EnableSecureUIAPaths"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.229 WN10-SO-000270"
      info        : "User Account Control must run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode, enabling UAC.

GROUP ID: V-220950RULE ID: SV-220950r1051037

User Account Control (UAC) is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. This setting enables UAC."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> \'User Account Control: Run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode\'

to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171r3|03.05.01b.,800-53|IA-11,800-53r5|IA-11,CCI|CCI-002038,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(d),LEVEL|2M,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-220950r1051037_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000270,Vuln-ID|V-220950"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"
      reg_item    : "EnableLUA"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CHECK
      description : "1.23 WN10-00-000105"
      info        : "Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) must not be installed on the system.

GROUP ID: V-220719RULE ID: SV-220719r958480

Some protocols and services do not support required security features, such as encrypting passwords or traffic."
      solution    : 'Uninstall \'Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)\' from the system.

Run \'Programs and Features\'.Select \'Turn Windows Features on or off\'.De-select \'Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.6,800-171|3.4.7,800-171r3|03.04.06,800-171r3|03.04.06b.,800-53|CM-7,800-53|CM-7b.,800-53r5|CM-7b.,CCI|CCI-000382,CN-L3|7.1.3.5(c),CN-L3|7.1.3.7(d),CN-L3|8.1.4.4(b),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|CM-7,ITSG-33|CM-7a.,LEVEL|2M,NIAv2|SS13b,NIAv2|SS14a,NIAv2|SS14c,NIAv2|SS15a,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.4,QCSC-v1|3.2,Rule-ID|SV-220719r958480_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-00-000105,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-220719"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_TEXT
      value_data  : "%windir%\System32\snmp.exe"
      file_option : MUST_NOT_EXIST
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "1.230 WN10-SO-000275"
      info        : "User Account Control must virtualize file and registry write failures to per-user locations.

GROUP ID: V-220951RULE ID: SV-220951r958518

User Account Control (UAC) is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. This setting configures non-UAC compliant applications to run in virtualized file and registry entries in per-user locations, allowing them to run."
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> \'User Account Control: Virtualize file and registry write failures to per-user locations\'

to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CCI|CCI-001084,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-220951r958518_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000275,Vuln-ID|V-220951"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System"
      reg_item    : "EnableVirtualization"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type            : AUDIT_POWERSHELL
          description     : "Check if no administrator accounts exist."
          value_type      : POLICY_TEXT
          value_data      : "No enabled local administrator accounts found."
          powershell_args : '$adminMembers = Get-LocalGroupMember -Group "Administrators" | Where-Object { $_.ObjectClass -eq \'User\' }; $enabledAdmins = foreach ($member in $adminMembers) { $userName = ($member.Name -split \'\\\\\')[-1]; $user = Get-LocalUser -Name $userName; if ($user.Enabled) { $user } }; if ($enabledAdmins) { Write-Host "Enabled local administrator accounts found:"; $enabledAdmins | Format-Table Name, Enabled } else { Write-Host "No enabled local administrator accounts found." }'
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.231 WN10-SO-000280"
          info        : "Passwords for enabled local Administrator accounts must be changed at least every 60 days.

GROUP ID: V-220952RULE ID: SV-220952r1051038

The longer a password is in use, the greater the opportunity for someone to gain unauthorized knowledge of the password. A local Administrator account is not generally used and its password may not be changed as frequently as necessary. Changing the password for enabled Administrator accounts on a regular basis will limit its exposure.

Windows LAPS must be used to change the built-in Administrator account password."
          solution    : "Change the enabled local Administrator account password at least every 60 days. Windows LAPS must be used to change the built-in Administrator account password. Domain-joined systems can configure this to occur more frequently. LAPS will change the password every 30 days by default.

More information is available at: https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/windows-it-pro-blog/by-popular-demand-windows-laps-available-now/ba-p/3788747 https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-overview#windows-laps-supported-platforms-and-azure-ad-laps-preview-status"
          reference   : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CCI|CCI-000199,CCI|CCI-004066,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|2M,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-220952r1051038_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000280,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-220952"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
        </report>
      </then>

      <else>
        <if>
          <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
            <custom_item>
              type            : AUDIT_POWERSHELL
              description     : "Password last set date for Admin account."
              value_type      : POLICY_TEXT
              value_data      : "PASS: Password age within recommended limits"
              powershell_args : '$admin = (Get-LocalUser -Name * | where {$_.Description -eq \'Built-in account for administering the computer/domain\'} | Select-Object *); $pwage = $admin.PasswordLastSet; $threshold = (Get-Date).AddDays(-@ADMIN_REFRESH@); if ([Math]::Abs(($pwage - $threshold).TotalDays) -ge @ADMIN_REFRESH@) {Write-Host(\\"FAIL:\\", $admin.name, \\"password last changed\\", $(if ($pwage) {$pwage} else {\\"never\\"}))} else {Write-Host(\\"PASS: Password age within recommended limits\\")}'
            </custom_item>

            <custom_item>
              type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
              description : "LAPS password age configured."
              value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
              value_data  : "@PASSWORD_AGE@"
              reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\LAPS"
              reg_item    : "PasswordAgeDays"
              reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
            </custom_item>

            <custom_item>
              type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
              description : "LAPS password length configured."
              value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
              value_data  : "@PASSWORD_LENGTH@"
              reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\LAPS"
              reg_item    : "PasswordLength"
              reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
            </custom_item>

            <custom_item>
              type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
              description : "LAPS password complexity configured."
              value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
              value_data  : "@PASSWORD_COMPLEXITY@"
              reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\LAPS"
              reg_item    : "PasswordComplexity"
              reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
            </custom_item>

            <custom_item>
              type        : REG_CHECK
              description : "LAPS name of administrator account enabled."
              value_type  : POLICY_TEXT
              value_data  : "HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\LAPS"
              reg_option  : MUST_EXIST
              key_item    : "AdministratorAccountName"
            </custom_item>
          </condition>

          <then>
            <report type:"PASSED">
              description : "1.231 WN10-SO-000280"
              info        : "Passwords for enabled local Administrator accounts must be changed at least every 60 days.

GROUP ID: V-220952RULE ID: SV-220952r1051038

The longer a password is in use, the greater the opportunity for someone to gain unauthorized knowledge of the password. A local Administrator account is not generally used and its password may not be changed as frequently as necessary. Changing the password for enabled Administrator accounts on a regular basis will limit its exposure.

Windows LAPS must be used to change the built-in Administrator account password."
              solution    : "Change the enabled local Administrator account password at least every 60 days. Windows LAPS must be used to change the built-in Administrator account password. Domain-joined systems can configure this to occur more frequently. LAPS will change the password every 30 days by default.

More information is available at: https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/windows-it-pro-blog/by-popular-demand-windows-laps-available-now/ba-p/3788747 https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-overview#windows-laps-supported-platforms-and-azure-ad-laps-preview-status"
              reference   : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CCI|CCI-000199,CCI|CCI-004066,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|2M,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-220952r1051038_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-SO-000280,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-220952"
              see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
              show_output : YES
            </report>
          </then>
        </if>
      </else>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type                 : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description          : "1.233 WN10-UC-000020"
      info                 : "Zone information must be preserved when saving attachments.

GROUP ID: V-220955RULE ID: SV-220955r991589

Preserving zone of origin (internet, intranet, local, restricted) information on file attachments allows Windows to determine risk."
      solution             : 'The default behavior is for Windows to mark file attachments with their zone information.

If this needs to be corrected, configure the policy value for User Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Attachment Manager >> \'Do not preserve zone information in file attachments\' to \'Not Configured\' or \'Disabled\'.'
      reference            : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|2M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-220955r991589_rule,STIG-ID|WN10-UC-000020,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-220955"
      see_also             : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23869"
      value_type           : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data           : "2"
      reg_key              : "HKU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\Currentversion\Policies\Attachments"
      reg_item             : "SaveZoneInformation"
      reg_ignore_hku_users : "S-1-5-18,S-1-5-19,S-1-5-20"
      reg_option           : CAN_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : USER_RIGHTS_POLICY
      description : "1.234 WN10-UR-000005"
      info        : 'The Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller user right must not be assigned to any groups or accounts.

GROUP ID: V-220956RULE ID: SV-220956r958726

Inappropriate granting of user rights can provide system, administrative, and other high level capabilities.

Accounts with the \'Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller\' user right may be able to retrieve the credentials of other accounts from Credential Manager.'
      solution    : 'Configure the policy value for

Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> User Rights Assignment >> \'Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller\'

to be defined but containing no entries (blank).'
